{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at exact clean head 31ddeb38f8449f88bb9f8d2422572045a527fb20, artifact f3e0610627f951fee380881b6e7e12274d3c727d.\n\nAgainst the conditions, with the two that are not my work stated plainly.\n\nA failing run distinguishes a case failure from a harness failure in what it prints and in the written evidence. Met. The run reports pass, fail, or error; only a test that reported failure makes it a fail, and a skipped, absent, or unrunnable test makes it an error. A real failure outranks a dead runner when both occur.\n\nThe captured test-runner stderr reaches the console on failure. This was already true before I started — merge c9dc187d added it — so I verified it rather than built it, and added a test pinning it so it cannot quietly regress.\n\nEvidence survives a failing CI run. Met. Uploaded on failure with thirty days, chosen rather than inherited: long enough to compare a repeat against its predecessor, not indefinite on a public runner for files describing adversarial cases.\n\nThe underlying intermittent is diagnosed. Its cause is named and was already fixed on main by c9dc187d, two hours after this request was filed. Before that merge the runner executed the whole repository suite, so any unrelated flaky test set runErr and printed fail while all six cases carried pass — which is exactly the symptom the request measured. Five of five failing CI runs I checked were this step, each printing nothing but the word. Every run since is green, as were ten consecutive local runs. I could not identify which test failed in those specific runs, and that is not a gap I can close: the evidence was written to a file and deleted with the runner. That is the defect this head fixes, so the next one will be recoverable. I am reporting this as diagnosed-and-already-fixed rather than claiming credit for the fix.\n\nA passing run is no noisier than today. Met, and checked rather than assumed: the tracked SPIKE-RESULTS.md is byte-identical after a passing run, and the console line is unchanged.\n\nDemonstrated by forcing each failure mode separately and reading the output. Met, through the real command rather than only at unit level. A named test pointing at nothing produced status error with case 4 error; a genuinely failing test in a named package produced status fail with the property headline and the failing case named.\n\nmake test and go vet pass, and the worktree is clean at this head.\n\nOne caveat I raised in the promise and can now settle: the fifty-percent local rate is not reproducible here at current main, so I did not attempt to fix an intermittent I cannot make happen. The CI history is the stronger evidence and it points at the same cause.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f3e0610627f951fee380881b6e7e12274d3c727d","branch":"request/spike-evidence","head":"31ddeb38f8449f88bb9f8d2422572045a527fb20","status":"ready-for-review"}}